## How parse-env can fail-open (threat model)
`c_parse_secret_env` is a **source-text** scanner, not a token stream:

1. **Wrong ident span** — `str_slice(src, start, k)` is exclusive-end.
   `k-1` drops the last char; `k+1` eats a trailer. Either way
   `__sec__` key ≠ sink IDENT → **FN** (Z1).
2. **Cap / drop** — a C `char buf[N]` or a short memcpy would truncate
   a long SECRET name. Same FN class (Z1).
3. **`oo_str_slice` OOB fail-soft** — runtime returns `""` when
   `start`/`end` are out of range (`chs_rt_str.c`). If the caller
   **skips** empty instead of `process_exit`, a bad slice **drops the
   tag** (Z1+Z2).
4. **Empty skip** — `if name != "" { put }` without `process_exit`
   accepts `// SECRET:` / `// SECRET:   ` / `// SECRET: !x` and
   continues. That is fail-open of the directive grammar (Z2).
5. **False directive** — matching `SECRET:` mid-line, in a string, after
   indent, or without the exact `// ` prefix tags a name the author did
   not declare (Z3 FP) or, worse, a later agent “fixes” line-start and
   starts tagging comments (Z3).

Z1 is the span class. Z2 is the empty-ident class. Z3 is the line-start
class.

